  {
    "submission": "submission-ip-probe.json",
    "summary": "Turn 72 found a startup-path defect in the last unread helper and filed it as a check.\n\nThe lead. Reading replica/defs/latency.go's local-address helper showed that it dials a fixed probe address (8.8.8.8:80 over UDP), discards the dial error, and then closes and dereferences the connection — so a failed dial becomes a nil dereference. The replica constructor evaluates that helper inline as an argument to the latency table, which means every replica construction calls it whether or not a latency configuration exists, so an environment without a route to the probe address cannot construct a participant at all.\n\nObservation. The harness calls the helper directly and wraps only that call in recover; the local construction run reports panicked true with a nil pointer dereference and no address produced, confirming the failure in this environment rather than inferring it from the source. Because the call has no inputs, the harness emits a probe-request prerequisite carrying a subject identity so the result can be correlated, and the plan asserts panicked == false with that identity shared.\n\nObligation and check (new claim C11-replica-startup-avoids-external-probe, bounded liveness, priority 2). Constructing a replica must not depend on reaching a fixed external probe address: when that address cannot be dialled, startup must still produce a replica or fail deliberately rather than dereferencing a failed connection. The claim is bounded to environments without such a route (isolated clusters, restricted execution sandboxes), excludes the latency table's parsing and delay arithmetic, and is grounded on the helper's source plus the constructor call site; the question is framed on the phase-1 record fact (a participant can only establish it after being constructed) with the durable-recorder and recovery-trigger behaviors as support, and a new binding locates the helper's declaration.\n\nContext. This is the tenth claim family and the first one located in the shared helper package rather than in the protocol logic; the codec, durability and recovery findings are untouched, and the map's latency surface remains the place that describes the helper's role. No requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened — the finding is scoped to environments the project itself may not target, which the claim states."
  }
